WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
API keys can appear in many places besides the obvious variable assignment.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:19.160
They may be stored in shell scripts, .env files, shell profiles, local configuration files, CI/CD variables, deployment manifests, client configuration, token caches, or temporary request files.

3
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.780
Some of these locations are intended to hold configuration.

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.480
Others appear because a script was written quickly and the key needed to be somewhere for the first version to work.

5
00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:32.520
Keys can also appear in places that were never meant to store secrets.

6
00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.220
Command-line arguments may be visible through process metadata.

7
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.000
Environment variables may be inherited by child processes.

8
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:47.060
Headers, query parameters, JSON payloads, and request files may be printed by verbose tools.

9
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:58.600
Logs, shell history, debug traces, support bundles, copied terminal sessions, and build artifacts may preserve sensitive values long after the script has finished.

10
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.760
A defensive engineer maps the key flow.

11
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.100
Where does the key enter the workflow?

12
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.720
Where is it stored?

13
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:07.340
Which command receives it?

14
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.760
Which child processes inherit it?

15
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.780
What logs are produced?

16
00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:18.040
Does a CI/CD job mask the exact value, and could derived or partial values still appear?

17
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.660
How long does the key remain available after the job completes?

18
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:25.920
These questions make exposure paths visible before they become incidents.

19
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.640
This mapping is especially useful in automation environments.

20
00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:39.580
A key may start in a protected variable store, pass through a runner environment, enter an API client, appear in a failed request log, and then be copied into a ticket.

21
00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:44.080
The original storage may be controlled, but the flow after injection may not be.

22
00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:50.020
API key handling is therefore about the whole lifecycle of the value, not only the place where it begins.

23
00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:55.720
Mapping the key flow makes exposure paths visible before they become incidents.
